#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81e7866b-f350-5882-9edc-b88eb5fa32f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363221} {:answer/id #uuid "a538d997-f541-5cd5-9f86-1941d4c76dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363222} {:answer/id #uuid "352bc6db-ff35-54f2-9eba-2b00a16a7c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363223} {:answer/id #uuid "ad80c4ff-d36a-5c66-a7cb-ace9512de8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363224} {:answer/id #uuid "15244f3a-2cb0-5b9b-b521-952867eaccdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15244f3a-2cb0-5b9b-b521-952867eaccdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363225} :answer-field/id #uuid "077f7bcc-c5d2-5fc1-8647-e14c43464e68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eefc328-7753-58ac-9913-74a31d74d60d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106849" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "566a627d-6250-5235-97ea-bf7cfd5fb50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363228} {:answer/id #uuid "b69d70c6-5dde-5a76-b2ae-464dcbfa38f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363229} {:answer/id #uuid "9d09635a-97bf-5b69-80d3-7edceff0716d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363230} {:answer/id #uuid "5159a1fd-3b5c-5fcb-b672-890b7b869546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363231} {:answer/id #uuid "d054bb3e-0bf9-5c05-9db0-0b54df07530b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "566a627d-6250-5235-97ea-bf7cfd5fb50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363228} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1fadc04-bb16-58d3-a85f-c3cd7bacda75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a361edc7-cba5-574a-bb24-756ca23de9e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106850" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-6)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3a6b157-db0e-5c17-a5f2-63e5739babd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363235} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4d0a4e-b259-570f-bac1-12341976fab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f801a8-5c41-5fd1-8b05-f0674139cfec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363237} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa7147-b056-504f-9da6-cb431270b806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363238} {:answer/id #uuid "90f3f40f-a1d3-5069-9e06-a05c5223b540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d4d0a4e-b259-570f-bac1-12341976fab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b8d237a-ded8-52db-909d-91ed6a327197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16e6e328-3f25-5a8b-a0a5-633d57921add" :question/math-academy-id "q-106858" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,$ ${u}_{33}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac3cb68-8607-575e-987f-2d0a58cd19ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363242} {:answer/id #uuid "917af3c5-e96f-5de4-a3d0-38bda9b0e1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363243} {:answer/id #uuid "85027f68-02b1-534c-93b9-e460998e9865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363244} {:answer/id #uuid "417587b5-7fe3-5d03-b31c-a1ad392b161f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363245} {:answer/id #uuid "0472be50-beb9-5479-a863-5f8eb83df9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "917af3c5-e96f-5de4-a3d0-38bda9b0e1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363243} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c461b2-ccd9-532f-95bf-2169ee654949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff095030-8971-5ea9-bda7-333f38e65d05" :question/math-academy-id "q-32533" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "657e00e3-a6eb-5a12-8116-19260005cc4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363249} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdb9ce8-d4fe-5ffc-87df-c38cbf1b2be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363250} {:answer/id #uuid "496c8b6f-90a8-5a2e-b6b9-ff4c4a1ae940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363251} {:answer/id #uuid "1222efe8-bcae-5e09-866b-924f9985f1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363252} {:answer/id #uuid "41d5b57f-6794-52ac-8c15-76972249df91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496c8b6f-90a8-5a2e-b6b9-ff4c4a1ae940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363251} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c2bf28-3148-59ca-9f1c-118bf76d0e83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7922748-04ca-53e1-9b10-ade53345b742" :question/math-academy-id "q-32722" :question/problem "Given that\n$K=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $K=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $K$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $K$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}K=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}K & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}K & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ K & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ K & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
